## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of non-trivial bit rearrangements across 128 bits without loops. The task blends endian concepts with mask-shift patterns, common in low-level data handling at scale.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit data as hi/lo halves
- Byte-wise reversal using masks and shifts
- Endianness-aware data transformations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline (actual C-like code uses uint128 struct)
static uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t t = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  t = ((t & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((t & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  return (t << 32) | (t >> 32);
}
uint128 endianSwap128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = bswap64(x.lo); r.lo = bswap64(x.hi); return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable endianness at compile time? 
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to implement a constant-time bit reversal to verify a binary palindrome using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome property in binary
- Bit-reversal networks with masks
- Branch-free comparison

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like implementation (illustrative)
int isBinaryPalindrome32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  x = ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  x = (x >> 16) | (x << 16);
  return (x == n);
}
```

